WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
My iPad, for
the most part, has replaced my sketchbook.

00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:06.566
And so in this video,
I'm going to show you three different ways

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.633
that I use my iPad for work.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.433
Hey, guys, welcome back to the Channel.

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:20.800
My name is Dami and I am an architect
in Beautiful Vancouver, B.C..

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.900
On this channel,
we talk about architecture and design

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.966
and also some tools and strategies
that can help us have more meaningful

00:00:27.966 --> 00:00:32.533
and fulfilling careers in architecture.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.666
Paper is still a very big part
of my workflow, especially

00:00:36.666 --> 00:00:40.300
when I'm working with my colleagues
or when we're doing design reviews.

00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:42.500
And I do think there are certain

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.666
things like paper
and a fat marker just can't beat.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.133
But after seeing some other architects
like Henry GAO or Eric Reinhold

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:56.700
Transform their workflow with the iPad,
I decided to get one for myself.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.700
And over the last year or so,

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.666
it's become
a really essential part of my workflow.

00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:06.900
I use a combination of procreate,
notability, click up

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.100
and PDF for work and I'm going to show you
how to use AI here.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.566
One of the ways
I maximize the use of my iPad

00:01:13.566 --> 00:01:16.666
as an architect
is with these graphic templates.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.966
I created these templates
and brush presets

00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:22.233
based on the things I use
every single day, and I've got things from

00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:25.866
people and tree brushes to scale of paper
templates.

00:01:26.166 --> 00:01:29.633
Basically, every time I start
a new project, I start with this

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.366
and it saves me tons and tons of time.

00:01:32.666 --> 00:01:36.033
All of these templates that I'm using in
this video are going to be available

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:39.433
at the link in the description.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:44.033
So the first way I use
the iPad is for design iterations

00:01:44.066 --> 00:01:48.100
as a design tool
in the earlier phases of the design.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.300
So when you're in the schematic phases
of the design, you don't really want

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.500
to spend too much time
fully developing one option.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.300
You want to try doing multiple iterations
and spend that time to develop multiple

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.233
rough options.

00:02:02.233 --> 00:02:05.533
And that's because you can have
a much better discussion this way.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.100
Like you can talk about the pros
and cons of each option

00:02:09.233 --> 00:02:13.333
and you can collaboratively pick
an option to develop more in detail.

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:18.100
Which is why in this phase, architects
tend to draw things out on paper

00:02:18.133 --> 00:02:22.033
because you can think faster and draw
faster than doing it on the computer.

00:02:22.166 --> 00:02:24.033
But the problem with this, of course, is

00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.233
that hands get floorplans
are not nearly as accurate

00:02:28.233 --> 00:02:32.166
as floorplans made on CAD or Revit
and it gets messy very fast.

00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:36.700
I actually used to spend a lot of time
just drawing and redrawing things

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.900
because even though the ideas
were worked out

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.666
well, it just didn't look clean
enough to be presentable.

00:02:43.766 --> 00:02:46.800
And I think we can all agree
the present ability of the thing

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.066
has a lot to do
with how your work is perceived.

00:02:50.166 --> 00:02:55.100
Then there's the scanning
and printing again, and there's just

00:02:55.133 --> 00:02:58.800
a lot of wasted time
and paper in this process.

00:02:59.266 --> 00:03:02.733
So now with my iPad, almost all of these

00:03:02.733 --> 00:03:05.500
inefficiencies have been resolved.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.333
I use procreate

00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:12.133
and I have a set of templates that I use
whenever I start a project.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.933
I start with my skill paper template,

00:03:15.133 --> 00:03:18.133
which I've set up in different metric

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:21.133
or imperial scales in different paper
sizes.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.366
I have it on about 30% opacity

00:03:24.366 --> 00:03:28.066
and you can turn it off before you print
or you can leave it on if you want.

00:03:28.233 --> 00:03:33.900
We typically print in tabloid,
so this is the one that I use the most.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:40.333
And because of these one meter grids,
I can get a much higher level of accuracy

00:03:40.533 --> 00:03:44.200
much faster, even when I'm just doing
a schematic layout.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.200
When I'm working on projects
with existing conditions.

00:03:47.333 --> 00:03:50.566
I would model the building and Revit
based on the existing drawings

00:03:50.566 --> 00:03:54.366
that are available and use the pdf
of the drawings as the base.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.933
The best thing about using the iPad
is the ability to make options with layers

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.733
and easily go back if you make a mistake
without having to restart or erase things.

00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:07.800
Once I've got the basic layout,
I have a set of skill

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.666
furniture elements that I can easily copy
and paste into the drawings.

00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:16.233
Or I use these stamp brush tools
which I have saved onto

00:04:16.233 --> 00:04:20.733
my Procreate app.

00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:25.100
When I'm developing the elevations,
I add color and tone with a brush set

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:27.000
that kind of mimics markers

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.366
and you can overlay them
and the lines get darker and thicker.

00:04:30.366 --> 00:04:35.533
And I feel like this
just adds a richness to the drawings.

00:04:37.733 --> 00:04:40.400
I've also been collecting different
background images

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.700
that I've been using
to stylize the drawings,

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.566
and I don't use this too much
when I'm working, but I find that

00:04:46.566 --> 00:04:50.533
it just adds another level of depth
when presenting it digitally.

00:04:50.766 --> 00:04:54.166
And you can also play around
with materials with this as well.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.166
When we move into 3D, as
soon as the design's been roughly modeled

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.166
in Revit, I take a couple of use of areas
that needs to be resolved,

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.333
and then I print a PDF of those views

00:05:05.366 --> 00:05:07.966
and I use them as a base for drawing over.

00:05:09.666 --> 00:05:12.000
And it's the same idea as the plans.

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:16.233
I don't want to spend too much time
detailing one option before

00:05:16.266 --> 00:05:20.100
discussing it with your team.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.733
You want to do a couple of iterations
to talk about the general direction

00:05:23.733 --> 00:05:27.966
of the building rather than spending
all your time in one option.

00:05:28.466 --> 00:05:33.433
The problem with when you're modeling
things on the computer, especially in 3D,

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.000
especially in Revit,
is that you tend to waste a lot of time

00:05:38.166 --> 00:05:41.800
trying to resolve details
that are actually not that necessary

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.600
and like trying to make the image
look nice.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:49.300
And I think there's something to be said
about the look of a 3D rendering.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:55.000
Like a rough 3D rendering, in my opinion,
looks a lot less presentable

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.166
than a rough sketch, like a rough hand
sketch, even for internal reviews.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:04.966
A rough hand sketch, by its nature,
feels more like a work

00:06:04.966 --> 00:06:08.766
in progress,
and it's more open for discussion.

00:06:08.766 --> 00:06:11.733
Because with procreate
you can just simply go back.

00:06:11.733 --> 00:06:15.933
If you make a mistake, you're sketches
end up coming out a lot more

00:06:15.933 --> 00:06:21.133
refined
and presentable than a sketch over trace.

00:06:22.233 --> 00:06:24.766
Another way I use my iPad is for site
reviews.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.800
Before I had my iPad, I would carry around

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.766
like a full set of drawings in one hand
and my phone in the other,

00:06:32.333 --> 00:06:35.033
and I would be taking photos while

00:06:35.033 --> 00:06:38.100
at the same time
trying to make notes on my drawings.

00:06:38.533 --> 00:06:42.900
And a lot of the times I would be flipping
through the pages and walking around.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.233
And if you're a very experienced architect
who knows the ins

00:06:47.233 --> 00:06:50.300
and outs of construction,
you might not need the drawings as much.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:54.166
But as a fairly new architect, you know,
I want to double check everything

00:06:54.166 --> 00:06:55.666
and document everything.

00:06:55.666 --> 00:07:01.000
As someone who's always been a little bit
self-conscious about going to slate Well,

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.666
because I don't always look like
I belong there.

00:07:05.566 --> 00:07:08.100
I'm always kind of looking for ways for me

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.733
to look more confident
and feel more confident.

00:07:12.266 --> 00:07:16.933
And so the iPad has been great for that
because I can have all my information

00:07:16.933 --> 00:07:20.100
right here and during construction,

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:24.466
you will inevitably come across things
that you don't really understand.

00:07:24.733 --> 00:07:27.000
But that's totally okay.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.400
And that's what your team's there for.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.666
But if you are on site alone
or you're still a fairly new architect,

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:39.400
one of the best ways to build
confidence is just to be overly prepared.

00:07:39.733 --> 00:07:43.866
Like, read all of the RFI
before you get to the meeting.

00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:47.600
And if you know that something's
being built while you're there,

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:51.166
try to anticipate the problems
before you get there.

00:07:51.466 --> 00:07:54.933
And if I don't understand
something on site, I'll say something

00:07:54.933 --> 00:07:59.233
like, Can you explain the problem
from start to finish?

00:07:59.233 --> 00:08:03.600
And you might feel
kind of stupid at the moment, but most

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.733
people are more than happy to walk you
through the problem, and it actually makes

00:08:08.733 --> 00:08:14.233
you look more thorough and like you're
taking ownership of the problem.

00:08:19.366 --> 00:08:20.200
I have my

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.466
drawings and I upload them to notability,

00:08:23.466 --> 00:08:26.433
which is basically just like a note
taking software.

00:08:26.566 --> 00:08:28.366
And as I'm walking around the site,

00:08:28.366 --> 00:08:32.233
I take photos
and I insert them directly into the pages

00:08:32.666 --> 00:08:35.966
and from there
I can take dimensions and write notes

00:08:35.966 --> 00:08:38.533
on the spine.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:42.033
I'll also pull up,
click up on my split screen,

00:08:42.466 --> 00:08:45.000
which is where I organize
all my project notes.

00:08:45.466 --> 00:08:48.866
And throughout the weeks
I will make notes on any concerns

00:08:48.866 --> 00:08:50.400
or things I want to check out.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.266
When I'm on site and when I get there,
I can go through the list

00:08:54.266 --> 00:08:55.533
and just check them off.

00:08:55.533 --> 00:09:00.500
Sketching directly on the photos
is also the best way for me to communicate

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.366
with contractors or consultants because
they don't really need pretty images.

00:09:05.366 --> 00:09:08.266
They just need the essential information.

00:09:08.266 --> 00:09:11.900
But usually they needed that information
like yesterday.

00:09:12.466 --> 00:09:16.766
So doing a quick sketch on my iPad
is usually the fastest way

00:09:16.766 --> 00:09:20.833
for me to send off that information.

00:09:21.766 --> 00:09:25.766
The most common way to use
the iPad is for markup.

00:09:26.633 --> 00:09:31.700
We used to print tons
and tons of sheets of paper to markup

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:35.466
construction sets, but now we can just
mark it up on the iPad.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:40.800
I use a simple PDF program to annotate
and I mean, yeah, that's pretty much it.

00:09:41.033 --> 00:09:44.200
If you're interested
in that design process and how I develop

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:47.766
concepts for real projects,
I'll leave the video right here.

00:09:47.766 --> 00:09:51.000
If you're interested in
any of these graphic assets that I use

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.266
with my iPad set up.

00:09:52.266 --> 00:09:54.366
I have a fully downloadable template

00:09:54.366 --> 00:09:57.900
from scale grids to trees to furniture
elements to people elements.

00:09:58.233 --> 00:10:00.966
And I'll leave the links to all of this
in the description.

00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:02.700
There are also other ways

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.800
that I use my iPad for architecture
and also for non architecture stuff.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.200
So if you have any specific questions

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.433
or want me to make a video on anything,
just let me know down in the comments.

00:10:13.633 --> 00:10:16.766
Remember to like this video
if you found it helpful and hit the bell

00:10:16.766 --> 00:10:17.966
if you want to be notified.

00:10:17.966 --> 00:10:22.933
As soon as I release a new video
and if you haven't subscribed, subscribe.

00:10:23.233 --> 00:10:45.733
And with that
I'll see you guys in the next video.

